-- Dodger — assertions about six objects, a generator, and a run you can lose.
--
-- Every case opens with `press a`, because every game opens on its title
-- screen. That is one line of ceremony in exchange for the title screen
-- being part of what the suite checks rather than something it routes
-- around: a game that never reached `play` would fail every case below.

test the rocks fall
press a
play 0.5 seconds
expect r1.y > 0
expect r4.y > 2

test a rock that leaves the bottom is recycled to the top
press a
play 6.67 seconds
expect r1.y < screenheight
expect r2.y < screenheight
expect r3.y < screenheight

-- The bug this replaced: six rocks on six fixed columns is a pattern, and a
-- pattern you can stand still in. A recycled rock has to come back somewhere
-- else, and the seed is what makes "somewhere else" the same somewhere on every
-- console and in every replay.
test recycled rocks come back in new columns
press a
play 6.67 seconds
expect abs(r1.x - 8vw) + abs(r2.x - 24vw) + abs(r3.x - 40vw) + abs(r4.x - 56vw) > 0

test every rock stays on the playfield
press a
play 15 seconds
expect r1.x >= 0
expect r1.right <= screenwidth
expect r6.x >= 0
expect r6.right <= screenwidth

test dodging is what scores
press a
play 6.67 seconds
expect score.value > 0

-- 120 ticks is two seconds, and the ship crosses the whole court in about 1.5 —
-- long enough to reach the wall, short enough to get there before the rocks
-- spend all three lives and the scene changes underneath the assertion.
test the ship runs to each edge and stops
press a
hold left for 2 seconds
expect player.x = 0
expect player.xdirection = 0

-- Standing still used to be a winning strategy, because the rocks fell down
-- columns that never moved. Now it is a losing one, on every console.
test a ship that never moves loses all three lives
press a
play 23.33 seconds
expect scene gameover
